# ec/tools/check_eq_guard_citations.py, wired into the cheap gate. Prepared here
# rather than landed, for the reason docs/ci/agent-gates-capture-claims.patch
# gives: `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` is copied from the agent-pipeline
# template and the pipeline's push token has no `workflow` scope, so a branch
# editing it fails at the end of the PR rather than at the start of it.
# `docs/agent-pipeline.md` carries this one across a re-copy too, as its item
# 15.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-eq-guard-citations.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# Added 2026-10-03 (issue #1129). `docs/findings/eq-guard-citations-gate.md` is
# the write-up and says what the tool does and, at more length, what it does
# not.
#
# Why the cheap tier: what it reads is the committed tree and the standard
# library. Five markdown files, one Python file, no firmware image, no Ghidra,
# no network, no assembler. It measures 0.14 s here (0.14-0.15 s over six runs)
# on 2026-10-03, against a cheap tier docs/agent-pipeline.md records at 5.9 s.
# That is one runner's figure and the ratio is the point, as item 6 says of its
# own.
#
# What it buys, and what it does not. It holds the line citations five prose
# files carry for one mechanism -- the `--no-eq-guard` flag, the `==` guard it
# turns off, and the two refusals that stop it being combined with `--check`
# and with the committed-output paths -- to the lines that code is on today,
# on every agent round rather than only in ci.yml's slow `tests` job.
# **The check is not unrun today**: ci.yml runs `tools/run-tests.sh` on every
# push and pull request, that runner discovers this tool's suite, and the
# suite's committed-tree case runs this tool against the committed tree and
# asserts it exits 0. So what this adds is the *tier*, not the coverage -- see
# docs/findings/eq-guard-citations-gate.md, which says so and shows the two
# perturbations that make the check red today. What the class is, is silent:
# a `:NNN` that has drifted onto a *different flag's help text* or onto *the
# other refusal* is not a broken link, it is a sentence that reads exactly as
# well and means the wrong line, and no reader and no other tool can tell.
# Nothing catches it by accident, so the check that catches it on purpose has
# to be run by something. Each anchor is a string of `xdata_register_map.py`'s
# own source resolved by reading the tool, so a drifted citation is reported
# with the anchor's current line beside it -- one edit rather than a
# re-measurement of a file that keeps growing.
#
# **It is not the general `.py:NNN` pointer checker.** It reads no file outside
# its declared five plus the tool, it does not walk the tree for citations, and
# it holds no pointer in them; #868, #869 and #870 own that class. The pins the
# same five files carry for *other* claims are counted and named as **declined**
# on every run, and a run that resolves nothing is a failure rather than a pass,
# because "checked nothing" and "found nothing" look the same from an exit code
# alone. Two more boundaries are visible in the tool's own output rather than
# assumed here: a citation inside a `>` block is skipped rather than read (a
# quoted correction is a denial of currency), and an undeclared bare `:NNN` is
# not attributed at all rather than guessed at.
#
# It is green as prepared, and that was measured before this patch was cut
# rather than after: the tool exits 0 on the tree this was cut from, and its
# suite (`ec/tools/test_check_eq_guard_citations.py`) passes. A patch that lands
# a red gate is the cheapest way to get a gate switched off, so the figure is
# here rather than assumed.
#
# Its two halves are placed differently and for different reasons, and
# `docs/findings/prepared-gate-patches.md` records both. The **function** goes
# after `check_registers_yaml()`, with the other `check_*` functions where a
# reader expects it: that boundary is free, no other patch in the set carries a
# pre-image window reaching it. The **`gate` line** goes below the "this tier
# does not run" note rather than beside its siblings, and that one is
# composition rather than tidiness -- every position in the `gate` list is
# another patch's context window, so a line in the tidier spot lands in some
# orders and not others. That failure is the one
# `tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py` exists to catch, because **each patch
# would still apply cleanly alone.** List order is cosmetic -- the `gate` calls
# are independent and nothing reads the order -- so the cost is a placement that
# looks wrong, the same trade `agent-gates-findings-frozen.patch` made for the
# same reason. That suite holds both halves in `REQUIRED` as well, because a
# re-cut that landed the function and dropped the `gate` line would apply
# cleanly, compose in every ordered pair, and pass `bash -n` and `shellcheck`
# while running nothing.
